BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2482@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T200713Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T151000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T152800
SUMMARY:Bridging the VM Boundary: Scheduling Passthrough via pvsched and sc
 hed_ext
LOCATION:Club H\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:sched_ext: The BPF extensible scheduler class MC · Josh Don\, 
 Vineeth Remanan Pillai · https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2482/\
 n\nAs production workloads increasingly transition to virtual machines for
  security isolation and resource consolidation in multi-tenant environment
 s\, traditional CPU scheduling faces a critical M:N preemption challenge. 
 The host operating system schedules opaque virtual CPUs rather than the ac
 tual workload threads. Consequently\, the host scheduler remains blind to 
 the varying priorities and latency sensitivities of the guest threads runn
 ing inside the VM. This leads to severe priority inversion\; for instance\
 , the host scheduler cannot differentiate between a vCPU running a low-pri
 ority batch or kernel system thread and one executing a latency-sensitive 
 task (such as a critical helper daemon) nested within the same VM. Consequ
 ently\, critical latency-sensitive work is starved\, and physical resource
 s are wasted under host CPU contention.\n\nTo resolve this\, we propose a 
 bidirectional VM Scheduling Passthrough architecture to bridge the VM boun
 dary from a scheduling perspective. This model relies on cooperative\, par
 avirtualized communication to enable host-side awareness and control:\n1. 
 Guest-to-Host: The guest kernel exposes scheduling metadata (potential sig
 nals include thread-level priorities and latency requirements) to the host
 .\n2. Host-to-Guest / Host Control: The host leverages these guest signals
  to either (a) intelligently prioritize the execution of a given vCPU\, or
  (b) in a more complete solution\, directly select both the vCPU and the s
 pecific guest thread running on that vCPU to execute (this latter proposal
  is more aspirational\, and would effectively give the host full schedulin
 g control over the guest\, voiding the need for guest scheduling or load b
 alancing).\n\nImplementing such a model requires a clean separation of mec
 hanism and policy. In line with upstream maintainer feedback\, KVM should 
 remain policy-free\, acting purely as the communication conduit. We propos
 e sched_ext as the ideal framework to house the scheduling policy\, and pv
 sched as the communication mechanism to share data between host and guest.
  Running the policy in a host BPF scheduler allows for rapid iteration and
  workload-specific customization without modifying KVM or the core kernel.
 \n\nCurrent upstream efforts\, notably the IBM patch series "Introduce cpu
 _preferred_mask and steal-driven vCPU backoff"\, attempt to address host p
 reemption purely from the guest side. In this model\, the guest monitors h
 ypervisor steal time and flags heavily preempted vCPUs as "non-preferred" 
 to guide guest-side task migration. While this provides a defensive mechan
 ism\, it is unidirectional and reactive. Furthermore\, as highlighted by u
 pstream maintainers\, this approach faces significant limitations: it risk
 s embedding scheduling policy within the hypervisor\, and in high-overcomm
 it scenarios where steal time is elevated across all cores\, the guest-sid
 e mask degrades\, leaving the guest scheduler with no viable execution tar
 gets.\n\npvsched (https://github.com/pvsched/) has undergone some discussi
 on on the list already\, and v3 is being prepared.\n\nIn this session\, we
  want to discuss:\n- Interface Design: The updates in pvsched v3\n- sched_
 ext Integration: How sched_ext can ingest these guest signals to influence
  host scheduling decisions\, and conversely\, how it can export host sched
 uling desires back to the guest
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2482/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2483@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T200713Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T152800
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T154600
SUMMARY:BPF-based Composable Idle cpumask Selection
LOCATION:Club H\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:sched_ext: The BPF extensible scheduler class MC · Emil Tsalap
 atis · https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2483/\n\nAll sched_ext s
 chedulers currently use kfuncs to manage their idle cpumask using a hardco
 ded policy provided by the kernel. This lack of configurability of the cur
 rent cpumask requires us to add policy through explicit masking operations
  directly in the scheduler code. This in turn leads to duplicating idle CP
 U selection logic across schedulers as it is difficult to factor it out.\n
 \nThis session discusses a new BPF-based subsystem for idle CPU mask selec
 tion. This subsystem abstracts the representation of the idle CPU set behi
 nd a common API that exposes explicit alternative policies to the user. Th
 e API is portable between schedulers and requires little code to adopt. In
 ternally\, the subsystem stores idle CPU information in arena-based data s
 tructures. Different structures (e.g.\, radix trees) provide different per
 formance/scalability tradeoffs and can be tailored to different idle CPU s
 election policies.\n\nTopics for this session:\n    - Overview of the idle
  CPU selection subsystem\n    - Data structures for representing the idle 
 CPU set\, tradeoffs for each\n    - Representing policy in a flexible\, ex
 tensible fashion through the idle selection API\n    - Using alternative C
 PU selection strategies as part of load balancing
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2483/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2484@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T200713Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T154600
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T160400
SUMMARY:Taming latency spikes caused by lock holder and lock waiter preempt
 ion
LOCATION:Club H\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:sched_ext: The BPF extensible scheduler class MC · Changwoo Mi
 n · https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2484/\n\nPreempting a lock 
 holder — or failing to promptly schedule a just-woken\nlock waiter — e
 xtends the serialized critical section and produces severe\ntail-latency (
 P99) spikes: degraded server throughput\, frame-time\nspikes and dropped f
 rames in games. Applications hit this on both\nkernel-space locks and user
 -space primitives backed by futexes and SysV\nsemaphores. Existing techniq
 ues help but leave gaps: proxy execution\naddresses kernel-mutex priority 
 inversion but not user-space futex waiters\nor counting semaphores\, and t
 ime-slice extension protects a detected holder\nwhile doing nothing for th
 e delayed waiter.\n\nWe define lock waiter preemption (LWP) as the dual of
  lock holder\npreemption (LHP): the scheduling delay a woken waiter suffer
 s before it\nruns\, which we have measured at 2–16 ms in production game
  and server\nworkloads. We will present these measurements and a prototype
  LHP/LWP\nmitigation built in a production sched_ext scheduler\, then open
  three\nchallenges for community discussion:\n\n- efficiently identifying 
 lock-related preemption from BPF\;\n- exposing synchronization state betwe
 en libc and sched_ext\;\n- designing lightweight scheduler-assisted lockin
 g that improves latency\n  without sacrificing scalability.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2484/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2481@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T200713Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T160400
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T163000
SUMMARY:Making proxy execution compatible with sched_ext
LOCATION:Club H\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:sched_ext: The BPF extensible scheduler class MC · Andrea Righ
 i · https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2481/\n\nProxy execution al
 lows a waiting task (the "donor") to donate its execution context to a mut
 ex owner\, enabling the owner to continue running while the donor remains 
 eligible on the runqueue.\n\nToday\, proxy execution and sched_ext are mut
 ually exclusive build-time options: a kernel cannot be built with both CON
 FIG_SCHED_PROXY_EXEC=y and CONFIG_SCHED_CLASS_EXT=y.\n\nThis limitation is
  problematic for Linux distributions and anyone who wants to ship a single
  kernel image while selecting features at runtime.\n\nAn RFC series propos
 ing support for this integration has already been posted: https://lore.ker
 nel.org/all/20260506174639.535232-1-arighi@nvidia.com/\n\nHowever\, severa
 l design questions remain open. This session aims to discuss those issues\
 , reach agreement on the overall approach\, and define a concrete plan for
  moving the integration forward.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2481/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2485@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T200713Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T170000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T171800
SUMMARY:Stickiness: Keeping tasks cache-warm in work-conserving LAVD schedu
 ler
LOCATION:Club H\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:sched_ext: The BPF extensible scheduler class MC · Gavin Guo 
 · https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2485/\n\nWork-conserving sche
 dulers prefer running tasks on idle CPUs\nimmediately\, ensuring no proces
 sing capacity is wasted while work is\nwaiting. In the lavd select_cpu pro
 cess\, when a task wakes\, the\nscheduler will do its best to seek an idle
  core and run the task over\nthere. However\, this idle-oriented CPU selec
 tion generally prioritizes\nidle cores over the cache-warm cores\, leading
  to more migrations\,\nmaking the tasks cache-cold\, and requiring L1/L2 c
 aches and TLBs to be\nrefilled frequently.\n\nThe work-conserving mechanis
 m works well in some scenarios. However\,\nthere are cache-sensitive workl
 oads such as edge routers using routing\ntables as in-memory KV stores. Th
 e migrations to the cold CPU have a\nglobal impact\, and the cache refilli
 ng evicts the other cache lines\nand pulls the new ones in\, making the ca
 che contention worse and further\nimpacting the tail latency.\n\n---\nWe w
 ill present our measurements and open the floor on topics\nincluding:\n\n-
  **Warmth estimation**: How long does L1/L2/TLB state realistically\n  sur
 vive on a CPU\, and could the kernel expose hardware signals\n  (e.g.\, PM
 U counters\, cache-occupancy registers) usable at\n  scheduling-decision f
 requency?\n\n- **The wait-or-migrate decision**: Waiting requires predicti
 ng when\n  a busy CPU will become available (remaining slice + queue servi
 ce\n  time\, or queue load?). Which metrics should a scheduler maintain to
 \n  make that prediction accurate?\n\n- **The scheduler/userspace interfac
 e**: Userspace structures (such\n  as allocator per-CPU caches) suffer hea
 vily from core migrations.\n  Should applications hint their locality need
 s to the scheduler\, or\n  should the scheduler expose warmth and migratio
 n state to userspace?
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2485/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2486@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T200713Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T171800
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T173600
SUMMARY:HFI plug in to scx_ext
LOCATION:Club H\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:sched_ext: The BPF extensible scheduler class MC · Srinivas Pa
 ndruvada · https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2486/\n\nThis presen
 tation explores how Intel HFI can be integrated with a sched_ext to improv
 e task placement and power-performance efficiency on hybrid Intel systems.
  HFI provides real-time hardware guidance on which CPUs are better suited 
 for performance- or efficiency-oriented work\, or which to avoid\, while s
 ched_ext like LAVD supplies an adaptive scheduling framework capable of us
 ing that guidance at runtime. By converting HFI output into scheduler-read
 able CPU hints\, the system can bias wakeup placement\, avoid degraded cor
 es\, and better match task type to CPU capability without hard pinning or 
 manual tuning.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2486/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2487@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T200713Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T173600
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T180200
SUMMARY:Automatic placement of accelerator workloads
LOCATION:Club H\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:sched_ext: The BPF extensible scheduler class MC · Balbir Sing
 h\, Lee Trager · https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2487/\n\nPropo
 sal\n\nOn modern multi-socket\, multi-GPU systems\, application performanc
 e is often limited not by compute availability but by poor CPU/GPU localit
 y. Today\, customers are frequently instructed to rely on strict node pinn
 ing (numactl) and disabling NUMA balancing in order to avoid costly cross-
 node memory accesses. While effective in some cases\, this approach can be
  suboptimal\, since it limits workloads to a subset of available resources
  and it also requires deep system knowledge from the user.\n\nIn this talk
 \, we will explore how GPU-aware auto-affinitization can be implemented di
 rectly in the Linux scheduler using sched_ext\, enabling dynamic and trans
 parent placement of CPU tasks close to the GPUs they actively use. We disc
 uss the challenges of integrating scheduler-driven task migration with NUM
 A balancing\, handling mixed CPU/GPU thread groups\, and avoiding resource
  over-concentration. Finally\, we present design principles showing how sc
 heduler-level techniques can outperform static affinity policies\, while s
 implifying the user experience.\n\nKey Challenges\n\nInteraction with NUMA
  Balancing\n\nNaively migrating a task closer to its GPU can backfire if t
 he task's memory remains allocated on a remote NUMA node. In such cases\, 
 migration may increase memory access latency instead of reducing it.\nKey 
 questions explored:\nHow can sched_ext and NUMA balancing cooperate rather
  than conflict?\nWhen should migration be deferred or paired with memory m
 igration?\n\nThread Aggregation and Shared State\n\nMany GPU-enabled appli
 cations use multi-threaded CPU components\, where:\nOnly a subset of threa
 ds directly interacts with the GPU\nOther threads share memory\, locks\, o
 r cache lines with GPU-driving threads\nMigrating only GPU-active threads 
 may introduce new inefficiencies due to cross-node communication among thr
 eads of the same process.\n\nWe need to explore strategies for:\n\nSelecti
 ve thread aggregation\, migrating related threads together\nAvoiding overl
 oad of a single NUMA node or LLC\nBalancing locality benefits against para
 llel resource contention\n\nResource Saturation and Fairness\n\nAutomatica
 lly clustering tasks near GPUs risks oversubscribing CPUs\, LLCs\, or memo
 ry bandwidth on specific NUMA nodes. The scheduler must therefore avoid ov
 erloading a certain LLC or NUMA node\, make optimal use of system resource
 s and maintain fairness.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2487/
END:VEVENT
